    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. also, is there any reason it would only happen at 4pm specifically? like, could it be a timer issue or something with the local power grid? it's a very specific pattern.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is plausible that the flickering streetlamp could be caused by a timer set on a faulty device inside the streetlight. Many streetlights are equipped with timers or seasonal dimmers, and a malfunction could result in erratic behavior observed by the residents. Alternatively, the consistent timing might correspond with street maintenance schedules. Streetlights and associated systems often undergo routine checks and maintenance, which could temporarily disrupt their operation and possibly be scheduled around common community times, including a period like 4pm when fewer people are on the street. \n\n\nHowever, an irregular flickering such as yours can be indicative of other issues like a faulty timer, electrical components, or even problems with the local power grid. Downstream service providers may execute timed maintenance that involves turning fixtures off or operating lower-powered at certain times as a cost-saving measure, which might result in the observed flickering. To find out precisely, contacting VicRoads or the local power supplier for an inspection can clarify the cause. In some cases, devices installed under street lamps are synced with the grid to ensure connections during less expensive hours, hence precisely avoiding peak consumption times. I would recommend reporting this detail along with your initial report as it could be useful for the investigation.",
